WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.458
- True or false and why:
You don't brush your hair.

00:00:03.458 --> 00:00:04.833
[Laughing]

00:00:04.833 --> 00:00:06.583
- That is false.
I do brush my hair.

00:00:06.583 --> 00:00:07.833
What do you mean?

00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:10.625
[Crowd cheering]

00:00:10.625 --> 00:00:14.000
I don't believe I can do
anything in this life by myself.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.667
- [Announcer:] Lob it in once
again, Durant single coverage.

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:19.208
Shoots over hard
and knocks it down.

00:00:19.208 --> 00:00:21.083
- Do you write music?

00:00:21.083 --> 00:00:25.042
- I write rhymes sometimes,
in the studio with just about -

00:00:25.042 --> 00:00:26.625
Not everybody,
but with a lot of people.

00:00:26.625 --> 00:00:28.000
- OK, where's the song?

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
♪♪

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.667
- Yo, this is KD
I'm about to "Take it There"

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:37.375
with Taylor Rooks.
I know she got a lot,

00:00:37.375 --> 00:00:39.500
a lot of deep questions
she want to ask me.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:40.750
I'm looking forward to it.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.542
[Crowd cheering]

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:45.750
- Kevin Durant!

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.833
[Horns honking]

00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:49.083
- [Taylor Rooks:] Thank you
for letting me spend

00:00:49.083 --> 00:00:51.208
your New York off-day with you.

00:00:51.208 --> 00:00:52.708
- No doubt.
- Is living in the city

00:00:52.708 --> 00:00:54.250
everything you thought
it was going to be?

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.958
- Yeah, it is.
You always hear

00:00:55.958 --> 00:00:57.583
the bad things about the city,

00:00:57.583 --> 00:01:00.042
and not so much the great
things that can happen.

00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:01.625
Every day I feel like I take

00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:03.125
a piece of New York City,
in a way, to work with me.

00:01:03.125 --> 00:01:04.250
- Yeah.
- So it's pretty cool.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.333
- Well, I know you love Jue Lan.

00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:08.208
This is one of your favorites.
- Yes. Yes.

00:01:08.208 --> 00:01:10.667
And you love all the food here.
You love Dara.

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:12.250
Now I know...
- My guy. Appreciate it, bro.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.375
- ...you're here so much
that Dara actually knows

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:16.833
your order -
Memorized.

00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:18.500
- Yes.
- What does he get?

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.583
- Kev loves the chicken satay
and the shrimp satay.

00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:22.000
Those are his go-tos.
- With the fried rice.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.083
- [Dara:] That's right -
fried rice.

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:24.958
Man, we go back almost,
what, 10 years now,

00:01:24.958 --> 00:01:26.208
coming back and forth

00:01:26.208 --> 00:01:27.417
to New York City, you know.
- [Taylor Rooks:] Mmhmm.

00:01:27.417 --> 00:01:28.708
- So I've got
friends here already.

00:01:28.708 --> 00:01:30.917
And I live right around
the corner. It was easy, man.

00:01:30.917 --> 00:01:33.750
- I met you when you were
an All-Star in Orlando,

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:37.000
playing for the Thunder.
We've been friends since then.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.833
What's the biggest difference
between the Kevin I met then,

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:41.250
and the Kevin you are now?

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.625
- [Kevin Durant:]
Just a natural evolution

00:01:42.625 --> 00:01:44.875
of being more comfortable
with myself.

00:01:44.875 --> 00:01:46.417
And I just removed
a lot of distractions

00:01:46.417 --> 00:01:48.292
that I was putting on myself,
I guess.

00:01:48.292 --> 00:01:50.208
- [Taylor:] What was the hardest
distraction to remove?

00:01:50.208 --> 00:01:52.583
- [Kevin:] Worrying about how
I'd be viewed after a game

00:01:52.583 --> 00:01:55.708
or how I'd feel after a loss
instead of just focusing on

00:01:55.708 --> 00:01:57.375
getting better
as a player every day.

00:01:57.375 --> 00:01:58.875
I stopped investing too much
in, like,

00:01:58.875 --> 00:02:00.792
just the outcome of the games

00:02:00.792 --> 00:02:04.083
and more so just kept it
at the work that I put in.

00:02:04.083 --> 00:02:05.667
- I don't understand this.

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:08.750
Why on Earth
did every person think

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.208
you were going to the Knicks?

00:02:10.208 --> 00:02:12.250
- It's the NBA.
Anything can start.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.958
- [Taylor:] Yeah.
- Any rumors happening,

00:02:13.958 --> 00:02:15.583
and stuff starts.

00:02:15.583 --> 00:02:17.250
It's just the nature
of the beast.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.417
- There was KD/
Knicks billboards.

00:02:19.417 --> 00:02:21.625
It was, like,
already graphics of KD

00:02:21.625 --> 00:02:25.125
and Kyrie and Zion,
like, in Knicks uniforms.

00:02:25.125 --> 00:02:27.708
- Every fan on social media
enjoys seeing

00:02:27.708 --> 00:02:30.458
what their team is doing
as far as free agency.

00:02:30.458 --> 00:02:33.667
- I was at the Nets game
against the Suns.

00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:36.542
You were pump faking.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:36.542 --> 00:02:39.333
- Fake dribbling,
fake shooting.

00:02:39.333 --> 00:02:41.167
And I was like, "I know Kevin.

00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:43.833
He is itching to come back
right now."

00:02:43.833 --> 00:02:46.292
- Yeah.
The culture of the game is, uh,

00:02:46.292 --> 00:02:47.917
definitely missing
from my life right now.

00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:48.958
- [Taylor:] Yeah.

00:02:48.958 --> 00:02:50.500
- [Kevin:] The team aspect of,

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.250
you know,
watching film every day

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:53.875
and just having
a goal to meet.

00:02:53.875 --> 00:02:55.583
As a basketball player,
I definitely miss that.

00:02:55.583 --> 00:02:56.542
So any chance I get

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:57.792
to be on the floor...
- [Taylor:] Mmhmm.

00:02:57.792 --> 00:02:59.375
- ...and kind of do
my shadow moves -

00:02:59.375 --> 00:03:02.125
I just try to envision what
it'll be like when I get back.

00:03:02.125 --> 00:03:03.542
- Are you itching?
Like, are you ready?

00:03:03.542 --> 00:03:04.875
- [Kevin:] Most definitely.

00:03:04.875 --> 00:03:06.458
My body's going to get there
eventually,

00:03:06.458 --> 00:03:08.958
but mentally, you know, I would
love to play again, for sure.

00:03:08.958 --> 00:03:11.625
- So is there any chance we see
you come back this season?

00:03:11.625 --> 00:03:13.000
- No, I don't think so.
Not for right now.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.583
- You don't think so or no?
- No.

00:03:14.583 --> 00:03:16.000
- Cuz those are
two different answers.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.500
- No, no.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.167
The best thing for me
is to continue to rehab,

00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:21.792
get as strong as I can,
and focus on next season.

00:03:21.792 --> 00:03:23.833
- OK, I want you to really
paint this picture for me.

00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:25.333
You've been asked
a couple times,

00:03:25.333 --> 00:03:27.000
but you haven't
really elaborated.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.625
- [Kevin:] Yeah.
- When you're back,

00:03:28.625 --> 00:03:31.583
what is this Nets team
going to look like?

00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:33.500
- I have no clue. I mean -
- You -

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:34.917
I know you think about it.
- I do.

00:03:34.917 --> 00:03:36.333
- You're a hoop head.
All you think about

00:03:36.333 --> 00:03:38.083
all the time is basketball.
- Yeah.

00:03:38.083 --> 00:03:39.250
- [Taylor:] So tell me,

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.417
when you're thinking
about the Nets squad,

00:03:40.417 --> 00:03:42.083
like, what's in your head?

00:03:42.083 --> 00:03:44.250
- [Kevin:] Just different
lineups that we can play and use

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.250
and with guys that work well
with each other's skills,

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:47.750
putting on the jersey,

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:49.583
and actually representing
Brooklyn on the road.

00:03:49.583 --> 00:03:51.667
That's going to be
an exciting time, as well.

00:03:51.667 --> 00:03:53.958
- I think that this has become
such a big part of your story.

00:03:53.958 --> 00:03:56.417
It's discussed so much.
Your time with the Warriors.

00:03:56.417 --> 00:03:58.208
Maybe the way
Golden State views you.

00:03:58.208 --> 00:04:01.042
It came up again recently
with Ja Morant and Steph Curry.

00:04:01.042 --> 00:04:02.833
Ja Morant said something
about Iggy.

00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:05.583
Steph posted a photo of Iggy
with his trophy.

00:04:05.583 --> 00:04:08.625
Ja Morant posts a photo of you
with your trophy.

00:04:08.625 --> 00:04:10.958
What do you feel like
Ja Morant was implying

00:04:10.958 --> 00:04:12.333
by posting that photo?

00:04:12.333 --> 00:04:13.917
- I don't know.

00:04:13.917 --> 00:04:16.792
I mean, there's always
been speculation

00:04:16.792 --> 00:04:18.625
about what my place
was on the team

00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:20.458
and what I meant to the team.

00:04:20.458 --> 00:04:22.125
And I don't think they got
anybody on the Warriors,

00:04:22.125 --> 00:04:25.208
like, upset that he posted
a picture of me.

00:04:25.208 --> 00:04:27.542
We all brothers, and we won
championships together.

00:04:27.542 --> 00:04:29.167
It wasn't anything more than

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:30.667
I guess just a little jab,
I guess.

00:04:30.667 --> 00:04:32.333
- I think that the narrative,
though,

00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:34.000
that was on social media,
separate from the Warriors,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.500
was like, they got those two
cuz KD was there.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.583
- It's narratives. They got
narratives for everything.

00:04:39.583 --> 00:04:40.917
There's a lot of stuff
the casual fan

00:04:40.917 --> 00:04:42.625
don't get about the game.

00:04:42.625 --> 00:04:45.167
So they make a lot of
speculation on what they see,

00:04:45.167 --> 00:04:46.792
and it's not
the whole picture.

00:04:46.792 --> 00:04:48.542
- The one thing you do say
when this happens,

00:04:48.542 --> 00:04:50.417
you're like, "We're talking
about basketball, not life."

00:04:50.417 --> 00:04:52.667
Like, nobody
is not friends after.

00:04:52.667 --> 00:04:54.333
- Yeah, it's not -
Yeah, I mean, cuz you hear

00:04:54.333 --> 00:04:56.542
all the trash talk in games.

00:04:56.542 --> 00:04:58.125
But it's like, we're all
a family at the end of the day,

00:04:58.125 --> 00:05:00.000
but we still compete hard
against each other,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.000
but we still want
to protect each other

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.750
when we're off the floor,
and we all respect what we do.

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:06.667
- I think the talk when
you were deciding

00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:10.083
what team you're going to go to
after Golden State was, like,

00:05:10.083 --> 00:05:14.125
do you feel like you have to
win a ring somewhere else

00:05:14.125 --> 00:05:18.292
to solidify this idea that you
can just win a championship.

00:05:18.292 --> 00:05:21.000
- I don't believe I can do
anything in this life by myself.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.333
I never felt that we can
win championships

00:05:23.333 --> 00:05:24.875
based off of only
what I do.

00:05:24.875 --> 00:05:27.542
I felt that way in OKC
when we had success

00:05:27.542 --> 00:05:29.167
and Golden State
when we had success.

00:05:29.167 --> 00:05:31.417
And it's going to have to be
the same thing in Brooklyn.

00:05:31.417 --> 00:05:33.500
A lot of people seen
that I left

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.000
a successful organization
and wonder why.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.833
For the most part, it's just
wanting to just make a change

00:05:38.833 --> 00:05:40.583
in my career.
It was simple as that.

00:05:40.583 --> 00:05:43.167
- It's so crazy to think what
OKC could have been, though.

00:05:43.167 --> 00:05:45.042
- Everybody says that, man.
- It really is.

00:05:45.042 --> 00:05:46.958
- Sometimes the business
gets in the way,

00:05:46.958 --> 00:05:49.708
and sometimes things
just don't happen.

00:05:49.708 --> 00:05:51.708
But that run we did have
was special.

00:05:51.708 --> 00:05:53.042
- It absolutely was.
- Going to the Finals.

00:05:53.042 --> 00:05:54.292
I ain't going to
never forget that.

00:05:54.292 --> 00:05:55.667
[Laughing]

00:05:55.667 --> 00:05:57.167
- It's interesting to hear you
talk about other teams

00:05:57.167 --> 00:05:59.042
you've been on
because you clearly have

00:05:59.042 --> 00:06:00.875
these incredibly fond memories
about the teams.

00:06:00.875 --> 00:06:01.667
- [Kevin:] Yeah.

00:06:01.667 --> 00:06:04.000
But the way it's painted
is that that isn't it.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.500
- Yeah. I mean, I'm just so open
and honest about the experience.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.333
I want the message to always be
without those experiences,

00:06:10.333 --> 00:06:12.250
I wouldn't be close
to who I am today.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:15.000
And they all made me,
and still embedded in my DNA -

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.542
OKC and Golden State.

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.417
And hopefully I can take this

00:06:18.417 --> 00:06:20.500
into my next experience
and be even better.

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:22.000
- I think, like,
a lot of players,

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.833
and really a lot of people,
say, you know,

00:06:23.833 --> 00:06:25.833
"I don't care
what people think."

00:06:25.833 --> 00:06:27.458
But I don't think
most people mean it.

00:06:27.458 --> 00:06:29.833
- No.
- Do you mean it?

00:06:29.833 --> 00:06:32.250
- Yeah. I mean, I feel like I'm
passionate about my art.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:34.542
I've heard Erykah Badu
say that.

00:06:34.542 --> 00:06:36.417
And I was. I feel that.

00:06:36.417 --> 00:06:38.500
- OK, so I play this segment
with a lot of people

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:39.875
that come on the show,
and it's called

00:06:39.875 --> 00:06:42.250
"True or False and Why?"
- [Kevin:] Mmhmm.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:45.333
- The best NBA players
come from the DMV.

00:06:45.333 --> 00:06:47.583
- Oh, my God.
- [Laughing]

00:06:47.583 --> 00:06:49.417
- True.
- OK. Why?

00:06:49.417 --> 00:06:51.792
- I look at regions that when it
comes to basketball players,

00:06:51.792 --> 00:06:53.917
is guys that fill
a specific role,

00:06:53.917 --> 00:06:57.000
and I feel like the DMV is made
for guys that get buckets.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.583
- What other cities, like,
do you put in there?

00:06:58.583 --> 00:07:00.292
I know it's a compilation
of cities, but -

00:07:00.292 --> 00:07:01.750
- Um, you can put Chicago
in there.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:03.042
You know, a lot of guys
from Chicago,

00:07:03.042 --> 00:07:04.625
their staple is defense,

00:07:04.625 --> 00:07:06.458
but they got some good
one-on-one players

00:07:06.458 --> 00:07:09.125
and good bucket-getters
out there in Chicago.

00:07:09.125 --> 00:07:10.667
- I think LA is more so

00:07:10.667 --> 00:07:12.042
guys that can do everything -
- [Taylor:] Yeah.

00:07:12.042 --> 00:07:15.125
- from pass
to score to athleticism.

00:07:15.125 --> 00:07:18.000
Just seeing so many influences
out on that side of the country

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.708
as well can definitely help
a young kid form

00:07:20.708 --> 00:07:21.958
who he wants to be
on the floor.

00:07:21.958 --> 00:07:25.167
So I look at all that stuff when
it comes to looking at players

00:07:25.167 --> 00:07:27.292
and what got me
to this point as well.

00:07:27.292 --> 00:07:29.250
- [Taylor:]
True or false and why:

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:31.333
You want to be a music
producer when you retire.

00:07:31.333 --> 00:07:34.458
- [Chuckling] False.
- [Laughing] OK.

00:07:34.458 --> 00:07:37.625
- I did a few years back,
not realizing how tough it is

00:07:37.625 --> 00:07:40.208
to actually become
great at that.

00:07:40.208 --> 00:07:42.500
Right now I just got
so much respect

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:44.125
for a lot of guys
in that position to say:

00:07:44.125 --> 00:07:45.250
- [Taylor:] Yeah.
-"I'm just going to do this

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:46.250
when I'm done," you know?
So.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:47.458
- Yeah, like,
it's harder than that.

00:07:47.458 --> 00:07:48.875
- Yeah, it's way harder
than that.

00:07:48.875 --> 00:07:51.292
- OK. True or false and why:

00:07:51.292 --> 00:07:53.875
people take you too seriously.

00:07:53.875 --> 00:07:55.125
- Um, true.

00:07:55.125 --> 00:07:56.833
The main topic around me

00:07:56.833 --> 00:07:59.000
the last few years
is moving teams,

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:00.917
and that hit
a lot of people hard.

00:08:00.917 --> 00:08:02.375
So now whenever I say something,

00:08:02.375 --> 00:08:04.542
it's getting blown
out of proportion.

00:08:04.542 --> 00:08:06.542
Sometimes I'm like,
"You can just ignore me."

00:08:06.542 --> 00:08:09.042
That's what my whole thing is.
You can just ignore me,

00:08:09.042 --> 00:08:11.125
but I understand
where it comes from.

00:08:11.125 --> 00:08:14.375
- True or false and why:
You don't brush your hair.

00:08:14.375 --> 00:08:15.708
[Laughing]

00:08:15.708 --> 00:08:17.500
- That is false.
I do brush my hair.

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:18.833
What do you mean?

00:08:18.833 --> 00:08:20.375
- [Taylor:] Well, people
always talk about that.

00:08:20.375 --> 00:08:22.000
- Yeah, on the basketball court,
I don't care.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.417
I come to the games, actually,

00:08:23.417 --> 00:08:26.500
just roll out of bed
and just go play.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.875
I mean, I'm on TV a lot
when I do these things,

00:08:28.875 --> 00:08:31.458
so I see why people make that...
[Laughing]

00:08:31.458 --> 00:08:33.333
But my whole thought process
is like,

00:08:33.333 --> 00:08:35.292
I'm really just rolling
out of bed to go hoop

00:08:35.292 --> 00:08:36.583
and come right back home.

00:08:36.583 --> 00:08:37.833
- Some of the memes are funny.

00:08:37.833 --> 00:08:38.917
- Of course, it is.

00:08:38.917 --> 00:08:41.292
-Yeah.
-It's funny, yeah.

00:08:41.292 --> 00:08:42.917
I got to laugh at it
if I'm going to laugh

00:08:42.917 --> 00:08:45.250
at all that other stuff
they put on there.

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.333
- Obviously before you got hurt,
there was this, what,

00:08:47.333 --> 00:08:48.625
like, this three-
to four-year stretch

00:08:48.625 --> 00:08:50.333
where you could
absolutely argue

00:08:50.333 --> 00:08:51.875
you were the best player
on the planet.

00:08:51.875 --> 00:08:53.625
- [Kevin:]
If you want to argue that.

00:08:53.625 --> 00:08:55.625
- Do you think
it could be argued?

00:08:55.625 --> 00:08:57.708
- Yeah. It could be argued
for a lot of guys.

00:08:57.708 --> 00:08:59.417
But, yeah, I feel like
I was in that argument.

00:08:59.417 --> 00:09:02.667
If you want to break
into that conversation,

00:09:02.667 --> 00:09:04.667
you got to do some
great shit in the league.

00:09:04.667 --> 00:09:06.250
You know, you got to really go
out there and leave your mark

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:08.208
and leave your stamp
on the game.

00:09:08.208 --> 00:09:09.667
And I want to be
in those conversations.

00:09:09.667 --> 00:09:11.417
I want to play
for a long time -

00:09:11.417 --> 00:09:14.000
to have longevity in the game
or playing in the league.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:16.500
That's because of what the guys
before me had laid out,

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:19.625
from the Jordans
to the Kobes to KGs

00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:21.458
to the Tim Duncans
to the LeBrons.

00:09:21.458 --> 00:09:23.000
Even now, I'm still getting
pushed by players

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.208
in the league now,
from the James Hardens

00:09:25.208 --> 00:09:26.708
to the Stephs to Giannis -

00:09:26.708 --> 00:09:28.167
guys that are going
to be legends

00:09:28.167 --> 00:09:29.833
and Hall of Famers
when they're done.

00:09:29.833 --> 00:09:31.333
It's pretty cool to know
that I've played

00:09:31.333 --> 00:09:33.458
with some of the greatest
players of all time,

00:09:33.458 --> 00:09:35.250
and they respect my game.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:39.875
- Now, unquestionable greatness,
the greatest - Kobe Bryant.

00:09:39.875 --> 00:09:43.333
How did losing Kobe Bryant
affect you?

00:09:43.333 --> 00:09:45.167
- Just can't stop
thinking about it, man.

00:09:45.167 --> 00:09:47.833
Kobe is somebody
that I competed against

00:09:47.833 --> 00:09:50.458
and was a teammate of
as well.

00:09:50.458 --> 00:09:52.667
I can go on and on about
how he made me feel

00:09:52.667 --> 00:09:55.875
as a person and as a player,
and I just - I love him.

00:09:55.875 --> 00:09:57.583
I'm always - it's always
going to be in my heart.

00:09:57.583 --> 00:09:59.583
Always going to think about him
when I go out there

00:09:59.583 --> 00:10:02.625
and play and perfect my craft,
and just try to do right by him

00:10:02.625 --> 00:10:05.125
by being the best person
and player I can be every day.

00:10:05.125 --> 00:10:06.583
You know, being on a team
with somebody

00:10:06.583 --> 00:10:07.708
and being their teammate,

00:10:07.708 --> 00:10:09.458
you really see them
for who they,

00:10:09.458 --> 00:10:10.792
who they really are to the core.

00:10:10.792 --> 00:10:12.792
It's hard for me
even to think about,

00:10:12.792 --> 00:10:16.500
you know, cuz, like, man,
I wish he was still around.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:18.667
- You said there were
little moments with Kobe

00:10:18.667 --> 00:10:20.333
that impacted you.
What are some of those?

00:10:20.333 --> 00:10:24.375
- [Kevin:] Just the first few
practices as Team USA in 2012,

00:10:24.375 --> 00:10:26.375
just his approach to practice,

00:10:26.375 --> 00:10:29.417
and then also how he played
in the games and his focus

00:10:29.417 --> 00:10:31.375
and what he wanted
to do out there on the court.

00:10:31.375 --> 00:10:33.292
He was a defender for us
and a shot-maker.

00:10:33.292 --> 00:10:35.167
So, like, to see Kobe
play a role

00:10:35.167 --> 00:10:37.458
outside of just being
the main scorer,

00:10:37.458 --> 00:10:39.208
it showed me that anybody can,

00:10:39.208 --> 00:10:41.500
you know, lock into a role
and perfect it.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:43.958
So it made me respect him
even more

00:10:43.958 --> 00:10:46.792
for just being a well-rounded,
well-versed player.

00:10:46.792 --> 00:10:49.417
As a hooper, I wanted
his aesthetic as a player.

00:10:49.417 --> 00:10:52.292
It was pretty sweet
to see his methods,

00:10:52.292 --> 00:10:54.125
and I didn't really get
to appreciate it

00:10:54.125 --> 00:10:55.667
until I got older
as a player

00:10:55.667 --> 00:10:57.250
and started to see,
"Oh, that's what he meant

00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:58.833
when he was doing it this way.

00:10:58.833 --> 00:11:00.792
Oh, that's how he felt
when he played in this game."

00:11:00.792 --> 00:11:04.833
So I'm always going to live
with Kobe in my heart.

00:11:04.833 --> 00:11:08.292
- Bryant attacking.
All the way. Dunks it home!

00:11:08.292 --> 00:11:11.375
Kobe down the middle
with authority!

00:11:11.375 --> 00:11:15.958
[Crowd cheering]

00:11:18.417 --> 00:11:22.250
- [Taylor:] Question for you:
What player from the '90s

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:25.125
would you most like to see
in the modern NBA?

00:11:25.125 --> 00:11:26.625
- Shaq, for sure.

00:11:26.625 --> 00:11:28.708
He'd be the most interesting
to see right now.

00:11:28.708 --> 00:11:30.792
- [Taylor:] OK. Why?
- [Kevin:] Cuz he's so big

00:11:30.792 --> 00:11:33.625
and agile and athletic
that he can run

00:11:33.625 --> 00:11:36.000
and play in a pace
and space that we do now,

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:39.042
but you couldn't play
small guys against him,

00:11:39.042 --> 00:11:41.708
so it'd be - he'd be the
ultimate, like,

00:11:41.708 --> 00:11:44.500
cheat code for the game.

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:46.750
- OK, so what's your drink
of choice?

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:49.333
- I got a little 1942 in here.

00:11:49.333 --> 00:11:51.500
- No - not on the rocks?
Just '42 straight with a lime?

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:52.833
- Straight. Yup.
- OK.

00:11:52.833 --> 00:11:54.500
All right,
let's talk some music.

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:57.208
I know that's another one
of your passion points.

00:11:57.208 --> 00:12:01.458
What five songs are
in your rotation right now?

00:12:01.458 --> 00:12:04.083
- Can I check my phone?
- Of course.

00:12:04.083 --> 00:12:05.833
- What I'm listening
to the most right now?

00:12:05.833 --> 00:12:07.833
- Mmhmm.
- "War" by Drake.

00:12:07.833 --> 00:12:09.125
- [Taylor, laughing:]
You love that song.

00:12:09.125 --> 00:12:11.500
- Every day - I listen to it
at least three times a day.

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:14.000
Uh, "Guess What," Russ,
Rick Ross.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:16.250
That's a dope song. You got to
check Russ' album out.

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:18.208
Fivio Foreign - Brooklyn,

00:12:18.208 --> 00:12:19.500
so I got to represent that.
- [Taylor:] OK.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:21.333
- And 2 Chainz,
"Virgil Discount."

00:12:21.333 --> 00:12:23.500
And I go "JackBoys,"
"Gang Gang."

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:25.167
- Sheck Wes...
- Yeah.

00:12:25.167 --> 00:12:26.833
- ...Don Toliver.
- [Taylor:] Mmhmm.

00:12:26.833 --> 00:12:28.125
- Uh, Travis.

00:12:28.125 --> 00:12:30.583
I'm really starting
to follow producers now.

00:12:30.583 --> 00:12:33.250
- OOh, who are some of your
favorites?
- Boi-1da, 40 -

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:34.875
Those two guys are
probably the -

00:12:34.875 --> 00:12:38.250
got me into, like,
wanting to follow producers.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:40.958
Um, especially, you know, one of
my favorite artists in Drake -

00:12:40.958 --> 00:12:42.375
they produce a lot of his stuff.

00:12:42.375 --> 00:12:44.333
Um, then into some new cats
that I heard -

00:12:44.333 --> 00:12:46.542
AXL Beats, uh, Rico Beats -

00:12:46.542 --> 00:12:49.167
There's a lot of guys
that I follow on Instagram

00:12:49.167 --> 00:12:51.833
that they post little snippets
of their music that I enjoy.

00:12:51.833 --> 00:12:54.167
♪♪

00:12:54.167 --> 00:12:55.917
- Do you write music?

00:12:55.917 --> 00:12:58.042
- Uh, I write rhymes sometimes.
- OK.

00:12:58.042 --> 00:13:01.667
- I got a little scrapbook
full of raps, I guess, yeah.

00:13:01.667 --> 00:13:04.625
- Do you ever record it?
- Yeah, I do.

00:13:04.625 --> 00:13:06.750
- Really?
- Yeah. I mean, it's fun

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.417
to get off
and get in the studio, yeah.

00:13:09.417 --> 00:13:11.958
That's a huge hobby.
It's underrated how many guys

00:13:11.958 --> 00:13:14.792
have their own in-home studios
as basketball players.

00:13:14.792 --> 00:13:16.667
I go to some big studios
sometimes,

00:13:16.667 --> 00:13:18.667
but most of the time I'm just
in the house creating stuff

00:13:18.667 --> 00:13:20.375
and having people come over.

00:13:20.375 --> 00:13:22.167
Me and JaVale McGee -
he's huge.

00:13:22.167 --> 00:13:24.458
Like, he has -
produces his own tapes.

00:13:24.458 --> 00:13:26.750
He has huge artists
on his mix tape, as well.

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:29.833
I feel like every team I've been
on had at least one or two guys

00:13:29.833 --> 00:13:31.792
that didn't mind
getting into the studio.

00:13:31.792 --> 00:13:33.375
So, yeah, it's pretty fun.

00:13:33.375 --> 00:13:35.792
- I had DeMar DeRozan
on the show last season,

00:13:35.792 --> 00:13:37.500
and he said he has a whole
mix tape...

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:38.917
- Yes.
- ...no one's heard except for

00:13:38.917 --> 00:13:41.708
Kyle Lowry, Drake,
and Kendrick Lamar.

00:13:41.708 --> 00:13:45.875
- When we was in, I want to say
2014 or -

00:13:45.875 --> 00:13:49.667
one of those, me and DeMar
got in the studio in Vegas.

00:13:49.667 --> 00:13:51.292
- [Taylor:] No!
- Me, DeMar,

00:13:51.292 --> 00:13:52.375
and Rudy - Rudy Gay.

00:13:52.375 --> 00:13:54.583
- [Taylor:] Rudy Gay raps?
- Yeah.

00:13:54.583 --> 00:13:56.250
You know, we all just
play around in there.

00:13:56.250 --> 00:14:00.167
- OK, where's the song?
Like, where - where's it at?

00:14:00.167 --> 00:14:01.667
- Nobody's going to
put no records out.

00:14:01.667 --> 00:14:03.250
We just doing it for fun.

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:04.417
- I'mma find those songs.
Trust.

00:14:04.417 --> 00:14:05.458
- No, you're not going
to find them.

00:14:05.458 --> 00:14:06.542
- I'mma find them.
-Nah uh.

00:14:06.542 --> 00:14:09.542
- I don't know where or how,
but you know I want mine.

00:14:09.542 --> 00:14:12.292
OK, so you have a song
with DeMar and Rudy.

00:14:12.292 --> 00:14:14.250
I think you and LeBron
have a song.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:16.667
- Yeah.
- Who else?

00:14:16.667 --> 00:14:19.375
- Uh, you heard me
and Stephen Jackson had one.

00:14:19.375 --> 00:14:22.375
A couple cats that
I knew playing ball.

00:14:22.375 --> 00:14:25.375
Myck Kabongo who played -
who was an undrafted player

00:14:25.375 --> 00:14:27.042
coming into the league
a few years back.

00:14:27.042 --> 00:14:30.542
I mean, I got songs in
the studio with just about -

00:14:30.542 --> 00:14:32.417
not everybody,
but with a lot of people.

00:14:32.417 --> 00:14:35.917
- OK, if you had a rap name,
what would it be?

00:14:35.917 --> 00:14:37.500
- [Laughing]
Probably "Easy Money."
- OK.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:39.125
"Easy Money" -
you can rhyme with that.

00:14:39.125 --> 00:14:41.958
- "Easy Money" or 'Slim Reaper."
One of those probably.

00:14:41.958 --> 00:14:43.833
Pick between those two -
or both.

00:14:43.833 --> 00:14:45.333
- OK, another top-five
question.

00:14:45.333 --> 00:14:46.875
- Yeah.

00:14:46.875 --> 00:14:48.667
- You could only watch
basketball highlights

00:14:48.667 --> 00:14:50.833
of five players forever.

00:14:50.833 --> 00:14:53.417
- I like that.
- Who are the players?

00:14:53.417 --> 00:14:54.917
- Kobe, Mike.

00:14:54.917 --> 00:14:57.208
Damn, that's tough.
That's tough.

00:14:57.208 --> 00:14:58.708
I watch film.

00:14:58.708 --> 00:15:01.583
Shaq, Magic, Bron.

00:15:01.583 --> 00:15:02.875
- OK.

00:15:02.875 --> 00:15:04.500
- If I couldn't watch
nobody else,

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:06.208
and they said I just had
to watch those five,

00:15:06.208 --> 00:15:08.708
I think I could be good
the rest of my life.

00:15:08.708 --> 00:15:11.208
- [Taylor:] My favorite game
that I play on the show.

00:15:11.208 --> 00:15:13.875
It is called "On This Day,
You Posted."

00:15:13.875 --> 00:15:15.292
- Oh, damn.
- OK?

00:15:15.292 --> 00:15:17.583
- I got a lot of posts.
- Yes, you do.

00:15:17.583 --> 00:15:18.958
[Laughing]

00:15:18.958 --> 00:15:20.417
May, 2010,

00:15:20.417 --> 00:15:22.125
"Twitter is better
than goin' to da club."

00:15:22.125 --> 00:15:24.000
- I was having a wack night
in the club that night.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:26.167
Nobody was dancing. Everybody
was just staring at each other,

00:15:26.167 --> 00:15:28.333
and I was like,
"This is what I come here for?"

00:15:28.333 --> 00:15:29.708
I guess I had an epiphany.

00:15:29.708 --> 00:15:31.292
When I have an epiphany
in my mind,

00:15:31.292 --> 00:15:33.000
sometimes I say it,
and that was one of those times.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:34.625
- Is Twitter still better
than going to the club?

00:15:34.625 --> 00:15:35.958
- Some nights.
[Taylor laughing]

00:15:35.958 --> 00:15:38.583
- August, 2009:
- Damn.

00:15:38.583 --> 00:15:42.292
- "I remember
when I got attacked by a dog

00:15:42.292 --> 00:15:44.000
walking to the bus stop.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:46.625
I got on the bus, and I
didn't have no lunch money.

00:15:46.625 --> 00:15:49.167
I got robbed by a dog."
- [Laughing]

00:15:49.167 --> 00:15:51.500
- Yeah, I remember that.
- Please tell this story.

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:55.292
- I was kindergarten, walking to
the bus by myself,

00:15:55.292 --> 00:15:57.625
and a dog came out
of nowhere.

00:15:57.625 --> 00:15:59.167
Ruff, ruff, ruff, ruff.

00:15:59.167 --> 00:16:01.417
I'm reaching in my pocket
when I got on the bus.

00:16:01.417 --> 00:16:03.042
"Damn, I can't eat today.

00:16:03.042 --> 00:16:05.042
It must have fell out
of my pocket."

00:16:05.042 --> 00:16:07.000
And I was like,
"I got robbed by a dog."

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:09.792
- The dog. Dog thief.
- The dog got me.

00:16:09.792 --> 00:16:12.000
- Last but not least,
my personal favorite.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:13.958
July 2011:

00:16:13.958 --> 00:16:16.750
"Bubble guts
woke me up out my sleep."

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:19.542
- I remember that.
- "I had to check my sheets

00:16:19.542 --> 00:16:21.250
for any slippage."

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.000
- Ahh, that's embarrassing.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:25.208
But I was really a comedian
at that point on Twitter.

00:16:25.208 --> 00:16:28.125
Or trying to be.
And I thought it was funny.

00:16:28.125 --> 00:16:30.875
- You didn't have
no bubble guts?
- Of co-

00:16:30.875 --> 00:16:34.375
If you have bubble guts in your
life, I mean, come on now.

00:16:34.375 --> 00:16:36.625
- The real question is:
Did you have any slippage?

00:16:36.625 --> 00:16:40.625
- No. Mnh-mnh.
Nah. Uh uh.

00:16:40.625 --> 00:16:44.542
Yo, can we cut that?
This don't need to be on...

00:16:44.542 --> 00:16:47.208
No, we don't need
to take it there.

00:16:47.208 --> 00:16:49.292
- With Taylor Rooks?
- Yeah, of course.

00:16:49.292 --> 00:16:52.292
- [Laughing]
- Of course. Shameless plug.

00:16:52.292 --> 00:16:55.042
- All right, KD, thank you so
much for coming on to the show.

00:16:55.042 --> 00:16:56.500
- No doubt.
- We got to cheers to that.

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:58.500
- Yes. To my fans.

